INT. HARBORVIEW VETERINARY CLINIC - NIGHT

RUIZ (V.O.)
Every war leaves a trail of paperwork. Nobody tells you the paperwork is the part that gets people killed.

Fluorescent tubes buzz over a waiting room that smells of bleach and wet dog, and Detective Mara Ruiz, fifties, rain on her shoulders, stands at the reception desk with a manila folder pressed to her chest while Dr. Femi Okafor, forties, blood on his scrubs, pulls a shutter down over the front window, and behind them four uniformed soldiers in damp fatigues fan out through the kennel hall, boots squeaking on the linoleum, rifles low, checking cages full of sleeping beagles and one furious cat.

RUIZ (V.O.)
Three tours, one medal, and now a vet clinic at two in the morning. That's how it ends for the kind of man we came to find.

THE SQUAD
Hall's clear!

RUIZ
Dr. Okafor, I'm going to ask you once.

OKAFOR
You have been asking since you walked in.

RUIZ
Where is Sergeant Hale?

OKAFOR
Sedated. Surgery two. He walked in with a shrapnel wound and a dog that was bleeding worse than he was.

RUIZ
A dog.

OKAFOR
A Belgian Malinois. Military issue, tag filed off. I stitched the dog first. The man made me.

RUIZ
And you didn't think to call anyone.

OKAFOR
I thought a dying animal outranked a phone call. Was I wrong?

RUIZ
You're harboring a deserter who is wanted in connection with eleven deaths.

OKAFOR
I'm harboring a patient. He has a pulse. That's all my license asks me to check.

RUIZ
Your license is going to be the least of your problems.

OKAFOR
Then arrest me after he wakes up. I'd like him to wake up.

RUIZ
You think he's innocent.

OKAFOR
I think he talks in his sleep. Under anesthesia he kept saying the same name, over and over, and it wasn't a woman's name and it wasn't a place.

RUIZ
Whose name?

OKAFOR
Yours.

RUIZ (V.O.)
There it was. Twenty years of keeping my past in a locked drawer, and a veterinarian hands me the key.

RUIZ
He's lying to you.

OKAFOR
Sedatives don't let you lie, Detective. That's rather the point of them.

THE SQUAD
Detective, we have movement in surgery two!

RUIZ
Nobody touches that door until I say.

OKAFOR
He'll tear his sutures if he stands.

RUIZ
Then he should have thought about that before he ran.

OKAFOR
Ran from what? Tell me that much. Tell me what a man like that runs from.

RUIZ
From me.

Silence. The cat hisses. Somewhere in the back, a steel tray CLATTERS to the floor, and Ruiz, hand on her holster, shoulders through the swinging doors as the Squad closes in behind her, lamps swinging across the kennels, Okafor following with his palms raised, and on the operating table the blanket lies empty, the window above it open to the rain, the Malinois sitting upright beneath it, waiting, staring at her as if it had been told she would come.

RUIZ (V.O.)
He didn't run from me. He ran to me. The dog was the message. The dog was always the message.

OKAFOR
What does that mean?

RUIZ
It means we are standing in exactly the place he wanted us to be.

THE SQUAD
Contact! Front entrance!

The lights die. Rain. Boots. Dark.
